### How do request-level retries work?

On any interaction you append `retryWhen(Retry.backoff(...))`, which retries with exponentially increasing delays and built-in jitter to avoid synchronized retry storms.

```java
package com.bank.rsocket;

import io.rsocket.RSocket;
import io.rsocket.util.DefaultPayload;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;

public class RequestRetryDemo {
    public Mono<BigDecimal> getBalance(RSocket rSocket, String accountId) {
        return rSocket.requestResponse(DefaultPayload.create(accountId))
                .map(payload -> new BigDecimal(payload.getDataUtf8()))
                .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofSeconds(1))
                        .jitter(0.5)
                        .filter(this::isTransient)
                        .doBeforeRetry(signal -> System.out.println(
                                "Retry #" + signal.totalRetries()
                                        + " for account " + accountId)))
                .onErrorResume(ex -> {
                    System.out.println("All retries exhausted, fallback");
                    return Mono.just(new BigDecimal("150000.00"));
                });
    }

    private boolean isTransient(Throwable ex) {
        return ex instanceof java.net.ConnectException
                || ex instanceof java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;
    }
}
```

```text
Retry timeline with backoff and jitter:
  Attempt 0: immediate
  Attempt 1: ~1.0s delay (+ jitter)
  Attempt 2: ~2.0s delay (+ jitter)
  Attempt 3: ~4.0s delay (+ jitter)
  Exhausted: fallback
```

### How do idempotency keys prevent double-debit?

For money-moving operations, every retry carries the same client-generated idempotency key so the server deduplicates.

```java
package com.bank.rsocket;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.UUID;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

public class IdempotentTransferService {
    private final ConcurrentMap<String, BigDecimal> processed
            = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public BigDecimal transfer(String idempotencyKey,
                                String from, String to,
                                BigDecimal amount) {
        return processed.computeIfAbsent(idempotencyKey, key -> {
            System.out.println("Processing transfer: " + key
                    + " from " + from + " to " + to + " amount " + amount);
            return amount;
        });
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IdempotentTransferService service = new IdempotentTransferService();
        String key = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

        // First call: processes the transfer
        service.transfer(key, "SAV2001", "SAV2002",
                new BigDecimal("50000.00"));

        // Retry with same key: duplicate detected, Rajesh not double-debited
        BigDecimal result = service.transfer(key, "SAV2001", "SAV2002",
                new BigDecimal("50000.00"));
        System.out.println("Result (no double-debit): " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
Processing transfer: a1b2c3d4 from SAV2001 to SAV2002 amount 50000.00
Result (no double-debit): 50000.00
```

### How do resumable sessions differ from request retries?

Resumption recovers the whole connection and its in-flight streams transparently. A request retry re-issues one interaction.

| Mechanism | Scope | What it recovers | Application awareness |
|---|---|---|---|
| Request retry | Single interaction | Re-issues one request | Visible (retryWhen operator) |
| Session resumption | Entire connection | Replays unacknowledged frames | Transparent (streams continue) |

```java
package com.bank.rsocket;

import io.rsocket.core.RSocketConnector;
import io.rsocket.core.Resume;
import io.rsocket.transport.netty.client.TcpClientTransport;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry;
import java.time.Duration;

public class ResumableVsRetry {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RSocketConnector.create()
                // Session resumption: recovers the whole connection
                .resume(new Resume()
                        .sessionDuration(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
                        .retry(Retry.backoff(10, Duration.ofSeconds(1))))
                .connect(TcpClientTransport.create("localhost", 7000))
                .flatMapMany(rSocket ->
                        rSocket.requestStream(
                                io.rsocket.util.DefaultPayload.create("RELIANCE"))
                                // Request retry: recovers individual requests
                                .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofSeconds(1))))
                .subscribe(p -> System.out.println(
                        "Price: " + p.getDataUtf8()));
    }
}
```

### How does a circuit breaker protect against overload?

A circuit breaker counts failures and, once a threshold is crossed, opens and immediately fails subsequent calls with a fallback.

```java
package com.bank.rsocket;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CircuitBreaker;
import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CircuitBreakerConfig;
import io.github.resilience4j.reactor.circuitbreaker.operator.CircuitBreakerOperator;
import io.rsocket.RSocket;
import io.rsocket.util.DefaultPayload;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;

public class CircuitBreakerDemo {
    private final CircuitBreaker breaker = CircuitBreaker.of(
            "balance-service",
            CircuitBreakerConfig.custom()
                    .failureRateThreshold(50)       // open at 50% failures
                    .waitDurationInOpenState(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
                    .slidingWindowSize(10)
                    .build());

    public Mono<BigDecimal> getBalance(RSocket rSocket, String accountId) {
        return rSocket.requestResponse(DefaultPayload.create(accountId))
                .map(p -> new BigDecimal(p.getDataUtf8()))
                // Retry inside the breaker
                .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(2, Duration.ofSeconds(1))
                        .filter(this::isTransient))
                // Breaker wraps the retry
                .transformDeferred(CircuitBreakerOperator.of(breaker))
                .onErrorResume(ex -> {
                    System.out.println("Breaker open or retries exhausted: "
                            + ex.getMessage());
                    return Mono.just(new BigDecimal("150000.00"));
                });
    }

    private boolean isTransient(Throwable ex) {
        return !(ex instanceof IllegalArgumentException);
    }
}
```

```text
Circuit breaker states:
  CLOSED   → normal operation, counting failures
  OPEN     → fail fast, no calls to downstream
  HALF_OPEN → probe with a few trial calls
  ↓
  If probes succeed → CLOSED (recovered)
  If probes fail    → OPEN  (still down)
```

### Why does ordering matter between retry and circuit breaker?

The circuit breaker wraps the retry so retries happen inside a closed breaker but stop entirely when the breaker opens.

```java
// CORRECT ordering: retry inside, breaker outside
rSocket.requestResponse(payload)
        .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofSeconds(1)))  // inner
        .transformDeferred(CircuitBreakerOperator.of(breaker)) // outer
        .onErrorResume(ex -> fallback());

// If breaker is CLOSED: retries happen normally
// If breaker is OPEN: fails immediately, no retries wasted
```

### What about fire-and-forget and retries?

Fire-and-forget has no delivery guarantee and no response stream, so it has no meaningful retry semantics. Never use it for money movement.

```java
// Fire-and-forget: best-effort, no retry possible
rSocket.fireAndForget(DefaultPayload.create("audit-login-event"));
// Use only for telemetry, logging, best-effort notifications

// For money movement: always use request-response with idempotency key
rSocket.requestResponse(DefaultPayload.create(
        "{\"key\":\"" + idempotencyKey + "\",\"amount\":50000}"));
```

### What are common mistakes with RSocket retry strategies?

The most common mistake is retrying non-idempotent requests like `debitAccount` without an idempotency key, risking double debits. Another error is configuring the circuit breaker after the retry policy in the reactive chain, so failed retries never trip the breaker. A third mistake is using fire-and-forget for operations that require confirmation, then being surprised when records are silently lost.

```java
package com.bank.rsocket;

public class RSocketRetryMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: retry debit without idempotency key");
        System.out.println("  .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofSeconds(1)))");
        System.out.println("  // Each retry debits again -- Priya charged 4x!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: include idempotency key in payload");
        System.out.println("  DebitRequest.newBuilder()");
        System.out.println("    .setIdempotencyKey(UUID.randomUUID().toString())");
        System.out.println("    .setAmount(50000).build();");
        System.out.println("  // Server deduplicates on idempotency key");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: circuit breaker AFTER retry");
        System.out.println("  .retryWhen(...).transform(circuitBreaker)");
        System.out.println("  // Retries exhaust before breaker trips");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: circuit breaker BEFORE retry");
        System.out.println("  .transform(circuitBreaker).retryWhen(...)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: retry debit without idempotency key
  .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofSeconds(1)))
  // Each retry debits again -- Priya charged 4x!

CORRECT: include idempotency key in payload
  DebitRequest.newBuilder()
    .setIdempotencyKey(UUID.randomUUID().toString())
    .setAmount(50000).build();
  // Server deduplicates on idempotency key

WRONG: circuit breaker AFTER retry
  .retryWhen(...).transform(circuitBreaker)
  // Retries exhaust before breaker trips

CORRECT: circuit breaker BEFORE retry
  .transform(circuitBreaker).retryWhen(...)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Always bound total retries and total time to avoid unbounded latency.
- Use jitter to prevent thundering herds when thousands of clients retry simultaneously.
- For request-channel sessions, decide whether to resume the channel or restart from a known checkpoint.
- In banking, queue transfers for asynchronous settlement when the breaker opens, so Rajesh sees "queued" rather than a hard failure.

```java
// Bounded retry with total timeout
rSocket.requestResponse(payload)
        .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))   // total timeout
        .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofSeconds(1))
                .maxBackoff(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                .jitter(0.5));
```
